💡 **SUGGESTION: guard the empty-bare edge in `isMachineAuthored`**

`stripBotSuffix` can reduce a login to an empty string: an author of literally `[bot]` (or `[BOT]`) yields `bareAuthor === ''`, and any config entry that is itself `[bot]` also strips to `''` — so `'' === ''` returns `true` and a non-login counts as machine-authored. `isMachineLogin` right above has the `if (!l) return false;` guard, but `isMachineAuthored` has no equivalent on `bareAuthor`.

Real-world risk is negligible (GitHub will never issue a bare `[bot]` login), so this does not block merge — but a one-line guard keeps the two predicates symmetric and fail-closed:

```javascript
var bareAuthor = stripBotSuffix(author);
if (bareAuthor) {
    var entries = machineAuthorLogins(machineAuthor);
    ...
}
```

A test for the `[bot]`-only author (and for a `[bot]`-only config entry) would pin the fail-closed behavior.

💡 **SUGGESTION: near-duplicate match loops in `isMachineLogin` / `isMachineAuthored`**

The strip-then-compare loop now exists twice:

```javascript
// isMachineLogin (case-insensitive):
if (stripBotSuffix(entries[i].toLowerCase()) === l) return true;

// isMachineAuthored (bare part case-sensitive):
if (stripBotSuffix(entries[i]) === bareAuthor) return true;
```

Only the case handling differs. A small shared helper (e.g. `matchesEntry(entry, target, /*caseInsensitive*/)` or a pre-normalized entry list) would keep the two predicates from drifting the next time the normalization rules evolve (this is the second iteration of the matching logic after gh-728's list support). Optional — the current shape is explicit and tested.

